public final class ImmutableOrder {          // Rule 1: final class
    private final String orderId;            // Rule 2: private final fields
    private final List<String> items;

    public ImmutableOrder(String orderId, List<String> items) {
        if (orderId == null || orderId.isBlank())
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("orderId required");
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.items = List.copyOf(items);     // Rule 5: defensive copy ON INPUT
    }                                        // Rule 3: constructor-only init

    // Rule 4: no setters anywhere in this class

    public String getOrderId() {
        return orderId;                      // String is immutable — safe to return directly
    }

    public List<String> getItems() {
        return items;                        // List.copyOf already returned unmodifiable list
    }
}

// Usage:
List<String> input = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Laptop", "Mouse"));
ImmutableOrder order = new ImmutableOrder("ORD-001", input);

input.add("HACK");                           // Does NOT affect the order
// order.getItems().add("X");               // Throws UnsupportedOperationException

System.out.println(order.getItems());        // [Laptop, Mouse]
```
*A Java `record` gives you Rules 1–4 automatically. But if the record holds a mutable field like a `List`, you must still apply Rule 5 in the compact constructor.*

