// ---- ABSTRACTION: the contract ----
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void process(Order order);  // caller knows 'what', not 'how'
}

// ---- ENCAPSULATION: inside each implementation ----
public class CreditCardProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    private String cardNumber;  // hidden — never exposed raw

    public void setCardNumber(String number) {
        if (number == null || number.length() != 16)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid card number");
        this.cardNumber = number; // validated in one place
    }

    @Override
    public void process(Order order) {
        // uses private cardNumber internally — caller never sees it
        System.out.println("Charging card ending in " + cardNumber.substring(12));
    }
}

// ---- Caller only depends on the abstraction ----
void checkout(Order order, PaymentProcessor processor) {
    processor.process(order); // no idea if it's card, PayPal, or UPI
}
```
*Swap `CreditCardProcessor` for `PayPalProcessor` and `checkout()` doesn't change a line — abstraction. Add an audit log inside `process()` and the caller doesn't know — encapsulation.*
